1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,419
part 3 chapter 4.

2
00:00:04,419 --> 00:00:09,099
He was much better. He was growing fatter and stronger every day, if it was proper

3
00:00:09,099 --> 00:00:11,200
to speak of days.

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,040
The white light and the humming sound were the same as ever, but the cell was a

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,740
little more comfortable than the others he had been in.

6
00:00:17,740 --> 00:00:19,659
There was a pillow and a mattress on the

7
00:00:19,659 --> 00:00:22,477
plank bed, and a stool to sit on. They had

8
00:00:22,477 --> 00:00:27,457
given him a bath, and they allowed him to watch himself fairly frequently in a tin basin.

9
00:00:27,457 --> 00:00:30,040
They even gave him warm water to wash with.

10
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:34,058
They had given him new under clothes and a clean suit of overalls. They had

11
00:00:34,058 --> 00:00:37,540
dressed his varicose also with soothing ointment. They had pulled out of the

12
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:41,619
remnants of his teeth and given him a new set of dentures.

13
00:00:41,619 --> 00:00:45,819
Weeks or months must have passed. It would have been possible now to keep

14
00:00:45,819 --> 00:00:50,080
count of the passage of time, if he had felt any interest in doing so, since he

15
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:53,439
was being fed at what appeared to be regular intervals.

16
00:00:53,439 --> 00:00:58,599
He was getting, he judged, three meals in the 24 hours; sometimes he wondered dimly

17
00:00:58,599 --> 00:01:01,240
whether he was getting them by night or by day.

18
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:05,259
The food was surprisingly good, with meat at every third meal.

19
00:01:05,259 --> 00:01:08,137
Once there was even a packet of cigarettes.

20
00:01:08,137 --> 00:01:12,878
He had no matches, but the never-speaking guard who brought his food would give him a light.

21
00:01:12,878 --> 00:01:17,378
The first time he tried to smoke it made him sick, but he persevered, and spun the

22
00:01:17,378 --> 00:01:22,840
packet out for a long time, smoking half a cigarette after each meal.

23
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:27,159
They had given him a white slate with a stump of pencil touch to the corner

24
00:01:27,159 --> 00:01:32,797
first he made no use of it. Even when he was awake he was completely topped Often

25
00:01:32,797 --> 00:01:36,819
he would lie from one meal to the next almost without stirring, sometimes asleep,

26
00:01:36,819 --> 00:01:40,477
sometimes waking into vague reveries in which it was too much trouble to open

27
00:01:40,477 --> 00:01:45,398
his eyes. He had long grown used to sleeping with a strong light on his face.

28
00:01:45,398 --> 00:01:49,540
It seemed to make no difference, except that one's dreams were more coherent.

29
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:53,619
He dreamed a great deal all through this time, and they were always happy dreams.

30
00:01:53,619 --> 00:01:58,419
He was in the Golden Country, or he was sitting among enormous, glorious, sunlit

31
00:01:58,419 --> 00:02:03,279
ruins, with his mother, with Julia, with O'Brien— —not doing anything, merely

32
00:02:03,279 --> 00:02:06,040
sitting in the sun, talking of peaceful things.

33
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:10,297
Such thoughts as he had when he was awake were mostly about his dreams.

34
00:02:10,297 --> 00:02:14,020
He seems to have lost the power of intellectual effort, now that the

35
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:18,398
stimulus of pain had been removed. He was not bored, he had no desire for

36
00:02:18,398 --> 00:02:20,259
conversation or distraction.

37
00:02:20,259 --> 00:02:24,700
Merely to be alone, not to be beaten or questioned, to have enough to eat, and to

38
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:29,200
be clean all over, was completely satisfying.

39
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:33,637
By degrees he came to spend less time in sleep, but he still felt no impulse to

40
00:02:33,637 --> 00:02:37,599
get off the bed. All he cared for was to lie quiet and feel the strength

41
00:02:37,599 --> 00:02:39,218
gathering in his body.

42
00:02:39,218 --> 00:02:43,180
He would finger himself here and there, trying to make sure that it was not an

43
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:47,200
illusion that his muscles were growing rounder and his skin taught her

44
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:51,218
Finally it was established beyond a doubt that he was growing fatter; his

45
00:02:51,218 --> 00:02:54,218
thighs were now definitely thicker than his knees.

46
00:02:54,218 --> 00:02:58,898
After that, reluctantly at first, he began exercising himself regularly.

47
00:02:58,898 --> 00:03:03,218
In a little while he could walk three kilometers measured by pacing the cell,

48
00:03:03,218 --> 00:03:06,520
and his bowed shoulders were growing straighter.

49
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:10,540
He attempted more elaborate exercises, and was astonished and humiliated to

50
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:12,939
find what things he could not do.

51
00:03:12,939 --> 00:03:17,080
He could not move out of a war he could not hold his stool out at arm's length,

52
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:20,137
he could not stand on one leg without falling over.

53
00:03:20,137 --> 00:03:24,340
He squatted down on his heels, and found that with agonizing pains in thigh and

54
00:03:24,340 --> 00:03:28,180
calf he could just lift himself to a standing position.

55
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:32,080
He lay flat on his belly and tried to lift his weight by his hands.

56
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:36,340
It was hopeless, he could not raise himself a centimeter But after a few

57
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:41,137
more days— —a few more meal times even that feat was accomplished. A time came

58
00:03:41,137 --> 00:03:43,180
when he could do it six times running.

59
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:47,378
He began to grow actually proud of his body, and to cherish an intermittent

60
00:03:47,378 --> 00:03:51,040
belief that his face also was growing back to normal.

61
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:54,457
Only when he chanced to put his hand on his bald scalp did he remember the

62
00:03:54,457 --> 00:03:59,200
seemed ruined face that had looked back at him out of the mirror.

63
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:04,058
His mind grew more active. He sat down on the plank bed, his back against the wall

64
00:04:04,058 --> 00:04:10,419
and the slate on his knees, and set it to work deliberately at the task of re-educating himself.

65
00:04:10,419 --> 00:04:16,180
He had capitulated, that was agreed. In reality, as he saw now, he had been ready

66
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:19,779
to capitulate long before he had taken the decision.

67
00:04:19,779 --> 00:04:23,137
From the moment when he was inside the Ministry of Love—

68
00:04:23,137 --> 00:04:26,919
—and yes, even during those minutes when he and Julia had stood helpless while

69
00:04:26,919 --> 00:04:32,080
the iron voice from the telescreen told them what to do— —he had grasped the frivolity, the

70
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:36,218
shallowness of his attempt to set himself up against the power of the Party.

71
00:04:36,218 --> 00:04:39,878
He knew now that for seven years the Thought Police had watched him like a

72
00:04:39,878 --> 00:04:42,398
beetle under a magnifying glass.

73
00:04:42,398 --> 00:04:47,439
There was no physical act, no word spoken aloud, that they had not noticed, no train

74
00:04:47,439 --> 00:04:50,619
of thought that they had not been able to infer.

75
00:04:50,619 --> 00:04:55,840
Even the speck of whitish dust on the cover of his diary they had carefully replaced.

76
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:59,919
They had played soundtracks to him showed him photographs. Some of them were

77
00:04:59,919 --> 00:05:02,378
photographs of Julia and himself.

78
00:05:02,378 --> 00:05:06,637
Yes, even.... He could not fight against the Party any longer.

79
00:05:06,637 --> 00:05:11,259
Besides, the Party was in the right. It must be so: how could the immortal,

80
00:05:11,259 --> 00:05:13,540
collective brain be mistaken?

81
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:17,919
By what external standard could you check its judgments? Sanity was

82
00:05:17,919 --> 00:05:22,000
statistical. It was merely a question of learning to think as they thought.

83
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,840
Only— The pencil felt thick and awkward in his fingers. He began to write down the

84
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:30,819
thoughts that came into his head.

85
00:05:30,819 --> 00:05:36,340
He wrote first in large clumsy capitals: FREEDOM IS SLAVERY.

86
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:42,340
Then almost without a pause he wrote beneath it: TWO AND TWO MAKE FIVE.

87
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:46,240
But then there came a sort of check. His mind is though shying away from

88
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,457
something, seemed unable to concentrate.

89
00:05:48,457 --> 00:05:52,000
He knew that he knew what came next, but for the moment he could not recall it.

90
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,797
When he did recall it, it was only by consciously reasoning out what it must be:

91
00:05:56,797 --> 00:06:02,740
it did not come of its own accord. He wrote: GOD IS POWER.

92
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:06,878
He accepted everything. The past was alterable. The pastor never had been

93
00:06:06,878 --> 00:06:13,000
altered. Oceania was at war with East Asia Oceania had always been at war with East Asia

94
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,200
Jones, Aaronson and Rutherford were guilty of the crimes they were charged with.

95
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:20,860
He had never seen the photograph that disproved their guilt. It had never

96
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:23,558
existed, he had invented it.

97
00:06:23,558 --> 00:06:27,340
He remembered remembering contrary things, but those were false memories,

98
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:29,919
products of self-deception.

99
00:06:29,919 --> 00:06:31,898
How easy it all was!

100
00:06:31,898 --> 00:06:35,680
Only surrender, and everything else followed. It was like swimming against

101
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:39,040
the currents that swept you backwards however hard you struggled, and then

102
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:43,477
suddenly deciding to turn around and go with the current instead of opposing it.

103
00:06:43,477 --> 00:06:48,700
Nothing had changed except your own attitude: the predestined thing happened in any case.

104
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:51,279
He hardly knew why he had ever rebelled.

105
00:06:51,279 --> 00:06:55,058
Everything was easy, except———!

106
00:06:55,058 --> 00:06:59,619
Anything could be true. The so-called laws of Nature were nonsense. The law of

107
00:06:59,619 --> 00:07:01,119
gravity was nonsense.

108
00:07:01,119 --> 00:07:05,740
'If I wished Brian had said, 'I could float off this floor like a soap bubble.'

109
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:10,058
Winston worked it out. 'If he thinks he floats off the floor, and divides

110
00:07:10,058 --> 00:07:14,919
simultaneously think I see him do it, then the thing happens.' Suddenly, like a

111
00:07:14,919 --> 00:07:19,840
lump of submerged wreckage breaking the surface of water, the thought burst into his mind:

112
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:21,878
'It doesn't really happen. We imagine it.

113
00:07:21,878 --> 00:07:24,040
It is hallucination.'

114
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:28,240
He pushed the thought under instantly fallacy was obvious.

115
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:32,137
It presupposed that somewhere or other, outside oneself, there was a 'real' world

116
00:07:32,137 --> 00:07:34,000
where 'real' things happened.

117
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,200
But how could there be such a world? What knowledge have we of anything saved

118
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:39,637
through our own minds?

119
00:07:39,637 --> 00:07:45,759
All happenings are in the mind. Whatever happens in all minds, truly happens.

120
00:07:45,759 --> 00:07:49,659
He had no difficulty in disposing of the fallacy, and he was in no danger of

121
00:07:49,659 --> 00:07:51,040
succumbing to it.

122
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:55,419
He realized nevertheless, that it ought never to have occurred to him. The mind

123
00:07:55,419 --> 00:07:59,740
should develop a blind spot whenever a dangerous thought presented itself.

124
00:07:59,740 --> 00:08:06,279
The process should be automatic, instinctive Crime Stop they called it a Newspeak

125
00:08:06,279 --> 00:08:11,977
he's set to work to exercise himself in crime stop He presented himself with propositions—'

126
00:08:11,977 --> 00:08:16,119
—'the Party says the earth is flat', 'the Party says that ice is heavier than

127
00:08:16,119 --> 00:08:20,080
water'— '—and trained himself in not seeing or not understanding the arguments that

128
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:22,000
contradicted them.

129
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,619
It was not easy.

130
00:08:23,619 --> 00:08:27,099
It needed great powers of reasoning and improvisation.

131
00:08:27,099 --> 00:08:31,297
The arithmetical problems raised, for instance, by such a statement as 2 and 2

132
00:08:31,297 --> 00:08:35,619
make 5 were beyond his intellectual grasp. It needed also a sort of

133
00:08:35,619 --> 00:08:40,297
athleticism of mind, an ability at one moment to make the most delicate use of

134
00:08:40,297 --> 00:08:45,119
logic and at the next to be unconscious of the crudest logical errors.

135
00:08:45,119 --> 00:08:51,119
Stupidity was as necessary as intelligence, and as difficult to attain.

136
00:08:51,279 --> 00:08:55,840
All the while, with one part of his mind, he wondered how soon they would shoot him.

137
00:08:55,840 --> 00:09:00,637
'Everything depends on yourself,' O'Brien had said; but he knew that there was no conscious

138
00:09:00,637 --> 00:09:05,200
act by which he could bring it nearer. It might be 10 minutes hence, or 10 years.

139
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:09,398
They might keep him for years in solitary confinement, they might send him

140
00:09:09,398 --> 00:09:13,477
to a labor camp they might release him for a while, as they sometimes did.

141
00:09:13,477 --> 00:09:17,439
It was perfectly possible that before he was shot the whole drama of his arrest

142
00:09:17,439 --> 00:09:20,200
and interrogation would be enacted all over again.

143
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:24,398
The one certain thing was that death never came at an expected moment.

144
00:09:24,398 --> 00:09:28,779
The tradition— —the unspoken tradition: somehow you knew it, though you never

145
00:09:28,779 --> 00:09:32,919
heard it said— —was that they shot you from behind: always in the back of the

146
00:09:32,919 --> 00:09:38,378
head, without warning, as you walked down a corridor from cell to cell.

147
00:09:38,378 --> 00:09:43,240
One day— —but 'one day' was not the right expression; just as probably it was in

148
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:48,218
the middle of the night: once— —he fell into a strange, blissful reverie.

149
00:09:48,218 --> 00:09:52,240
He was walking down the corridor, waiting for the bullet. He knew that it was

150
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:53,680
coming in another moment.

151
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:56,860
Everything was settled, smoothed out, reconciled.

152
00:09:56,860 --> 00:10:01,599
There were no more doubts, no more arguments, no more pain, no more fear. His

153
00:10:01,599 --> 00:10:05,619
body was healthy and strong. He walked easily, with the joy of movement and with

154
00:10:05,619 --> 00:10:07,779
a feeling of walking in sunlight.

155
00:10:07,779 --> 00:10:11,137
He was not any longer in the narrow white corridors in the Ministry of Love,

156
00:10:11,137 --> 00:10:16,180
he was in the enormous sunlit passage, a kilometer wide, down which he had seemed

157
00:10:16,180 --> 00:10:18,340
to walk in the delirium induced by drugs.

158
00:10:18,340 --> 00:10:22,840
He was in the Golden Country, following the foot track across the old rabbit

159
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:27,457
cropped pasture. He could feel the short springy turf under his feet into the

160
00:10:27,457 --> 00:10:29,680
gentle sunshine on his face.

161
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:33,279
At the edge of the field were the elm trees, faintly stirring, and somewhere

162
00:10:33,279 --> 00:10:38,080
beyond that was the stream where the day slay in the green pools under the willows.

163
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:43,058
Suddenly he started up with a shock of horror. The sweat broke out on his backbone.

164
00:10:43,058 --> 00:10:46,240
He had heard himself cry aloud: 'Julia!

165
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:47,797
Julia!

166
00:10:47,797 --> 00:10:51,457
Julia, my love! Julia!'

167
00:10:51,457 --> 00:10:55,297
For a moment he had had an overwhelming hallucination of her presence.

168
00:10:55,297 --> 00:10:59,500
She had seemed to be not merely with him, but inside him.

169
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:03,159
It was as though she had got into the texture of his skin.

170
00:11:03,159 --> 00:11:06,520
In that moment he had loved her far more than he had ever done when they were

171
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:08,200
together and free.

172
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:12,939
Also he knew that somewhere or other she was still alive and needed his help.

173
00:11:12,939 --> 00:11:18,099
He lay back on the bed and tried to compose himself. What had he done? How

174
00:11:18,099 --> 00:11:22,500
many years had he added to his servitude by that moment of weakness?

175
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:26,439
In another moment he would hear the of boots outside.

176
00:11:26,439 --> 00:11:31,058
They could not let such an outburst go unpunished. They would know now, if they

177
00:11:31,058 --> 00:11:34,957
had not known before, that he was breaking the agreement he had made with them.

178
00:11:34,957 --> 00:11:38,439
He obeyed the Party, but he still hated the Party.

179
00:11:38,439 --> 00:11:43,419
In the old days he had hidden a heretical mind beneath an appearance of conformity.

180
00:11:43,419 --> 00:11:48,040
Now he had retreated a step further: in the mind he had surrendered, but he had

181
00:11:48,040 --> 00:11:52,957
hoped to keep the inner heart in violet He knew that he was in the wrong, but he

182
00:11:52,957 --> 00:11:54,819
preferred to be in the wrong.

183
00:11:54,819 --> 00:11:59,259
They would understand that— —O'Brien would understand it. It was all confessed in

184
00:11:59,259 --> 00:12:01,718
that single foolish cry.

185
00:12:01,718 --> 00:12:06,878
He would have to start all over again. It might take years. He ran a hand over his

186
00:12:06,878 --> 00:12:10,119
face, trying to familiarize himself with the new shape.

187
00:12:10,119 --> 00:12:14,919
There were deep furrows in the cheeks, the cheekbones felt sharp, the nose flattened.

188
00:12:14,919 --> 00:12:20,378
Besides, since last seeing himself in the glass he had been given a complete new set of teeth.

189
00:12:20,378 --> 00:12:25,000
It was not easy to preserve inscrutability when you did not know what your face looked like.

190
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,297
In any case, mere control of the features was not enough.

191
00:12:28,297 --> 00:12:31,898
For the first time he perceived that if he wanted to keep a secret you must also

192
00:12:31,898 --> 00:12:33,398
hide it from yourself.

193
00:12:33,398 --> 00:12:37,297
You must know all the while that it is there, but until it is needed you must

194
00:12:37,297 --> 00:12:41,860
never let it emerge into your consciousness in any shape that could be given a name.

195
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:46,898
From now onwards he must not only think right; he must feel right, dream right.

196
00:12:46,898 --> 00:12:50,319
And all the while he must keep his hatred locked up inside him like a ball

197
00:12:50,319 --> 00:12:54,398
of matter which was part of himself and yet unconnected with the rest of him, a

198
00:12:54,398 --> 00:12:57,119
kind of cyst.

199
00:12:57,457 --> 00:12:59,439
One day they would decide to shoot him.

200
00:12:59,439 --> 00:13:02,919
You could not tell when it would happen, but a few seconds beforehand it should

201
00:13:02,919 --> 00:13:04,540
be possible to guess.

202
00:13:04,540 --> 00:13:09,759
It was always from behind, walking down a corridor 10 seconds would be enough. In

203
00:13:09,759 --> 00:13:12,398
that time the world inside him could turn over.

204
00:13:12,398 --> 00:13:16,718
And then suddenly, without a word uttered, without a check in his step, without the

205
00:13:16,718 --> 00:13:20,558
changing of a line in his face— —suddenly the camouflage would be down

206
00:13:20,558 --> 00:13:24,340
and bang! would go the batteries of his hatred.

207
00:13:24,340 --> 00:13:28,419
Hatred would fill him like an enormous roaring flame. And almost in the same

208
00:13:28,419 --> 00:13:31,659
instant bang! would go the bullet, too late, or too early.

209
00:13:31,659 --> 00:13:35,137
They would have blown his brain to pieces before they could reclaim it.

210
00:13:35,137 --> 00:13:40,058
The heretical thought would be unpunished, unrepented, out of their reach forever

211
00:13:40,058 --> 00:13:42,939
They would have blown a hole in their own perfection.

212
00:13:42,939 --> 00:13:46,477
To die hating them, that was freedom.

213
00:13:46,477 --> 00:13:51,398
He shut his eyes. It was more difficult than accepting an intellectual discipline.

214
00:13:51,398 --> 00:13:55,360
It was a question of degrading himself, mutilating himself.

215
00:13:55,360 --> 00:13:59,680
He had got to plunge into the filthiest of filth. What was the most horrible,

216
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:01,477
sickening thing of all?

217
00:14:01,477 --> 00:14:05,977
He thought of Big Brother. The enormous face (because of constantly seeing it on

218
00:14:05,977 --> 00:14:09,759
posters he always thought of it as being a meter wide), with its heavy black

219
00:14:09,759 --> 00:14:13,779
mustache in the eyes that followed you to and fro, seemed to float into his mind

220
00:14:13,779 --> 00:14:15,520
of its own accord.

221
00:14:15,520 --> 00:14:19,860
What were his true feelings towards Big Brother?

222
00:14:20,020 --> 00:14:24,159
There was a heavy trap of boots in the passage. The steel door swung open with a

223
00:14:24,159 --> 00:14:26,279
clang. O'Brien walked into the cell.

224
00:14:26,279 --> 00:14:31,540
Behind him with a wax and faced officer and the black uniformed guards.

225
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:34,240
'Get up,' said O'Brien. 'Come here.'

226
00:14:34,240 --> 00:14:36,398
Winston stood opposite him.

227
00:14:36,398 --> 00:14:41,558
O'Brien took Winston's shoulders between his strong hands and looked at him closely.

228
00:14:41,558 --> 00:14:45,580
'You have had thoughts of deceiving me,' he said. 'That was stupid.

229
00:14:45,580 --> 00:14:48,218
Stand up straighter. Look me in the face.'

230
00:14:48,218 --> 00:14:53,680
He paused, and went on in a gentler tone: 'You are improving.

231
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:57,637
Intellectually there is very little wrong with you. It is only emotionally

232
00:14:57,637 --> 00:14:59,977
that you have failed to make progress.

233
00:14:59,977 --> 00:15:06,099
Tell me, Winston— —and remember, no lies: you know that I am always able to detect a lie—

234
00:15:06,099 --> 00:15:10,058
—tell me, what are your true feelings towards Big Brother?'

235
00:15:10,058 --> 00:15:12,040
'I hate him.'

236
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:13,599
'You hate him.

237
00:15:13,599 --> 00:15:18,279
Good. Then the time has come for you to take the last step. You must love Big

238
00:15:18,279 --> 00:15:23,378
Brother. It is not enough to obey him: you must love him.'

239
00:15:23,378 --> 00:15:27,279
He released Winston with a little push towards the guards.

240
00:15:27,279 --> 00:15:30,000
'Room 101,' he said
